    correctChoiceId: 'choice-d',
    explanation: 'KQL is the native query language for Eventhouse and directly expresses this requirement with where, summarize, and bin(). Eventhouse can also expose a read-only T-SQL query mode for supported queries, but that does not make DAX or SQL stored procedures valid in a KQL query tab. Fabric Warehouse uses its supported T-SQL surface for relational analytics, Lakehouse SQL analytics endpoints are read-only over Delta tables, and semantic model calculations use DAX.',
    alternativeExplanations: {
      'choice-a': 'The Eventhouse query editor can run supported T-SQL SELECT queries and translate SQL queries to KQL. Creating a stored procedure is not supported by that query surface, and SQL statements entered in the KQL tab are not interpreted as KQL.',
      'choice-b': 'DAX calculated tables operate on data already loaded into a Power BI semantic model and are recalculated during model refresh. DAX does not execute in an Eventhouse KQL queryset and adds a separate model-refresh boundary.',
      'choice-c': 'Fabric notebooks can connect to KQL databases for programmatic analysis, and Spark is suited to distributed processing. Copying the data and scheduling batch work adds an unnecessary engine and latency boundary when the required interactive query is directly expressible in KQL.'
    },
    skillArea: 'Prepare Data / Query Language Distinction (KQL vs SQL vs DAX)',
    examTakeaway: 'Match the query language to the engine: KQL for Eventhouses/telemetry, T-SQL for Warehouses/SQL endpoints, and DAX for semantic models.'
