    correctChoiceId: 'choice-d',
    explanation: 'RLS filters the rows a role can access but does not hide tables, columns, or their metadata. OLS can assign metadataPermission none to BaseSalary and BonusAmount so those columns appear not to exist for the manager role. Keep the regional RLS rule for row filtering and keep managers in the workspace Viewer role, because semantic-model RLS and OLS do not restrict users with Admin, Member, or Contributor edit permissions.',
    alternativeExplanations: {
      'choice-a': 'An RLS table-filter expression determines which employee rows the role can return. Adding a salary condition reduces the permitted row set but leaves the BaseSalary and BonusAmount objects discoverable and still permits calculations over the remaining rows.',
      'choice-b': 'Hidden fields and perspectives simplify the authoring experience by presenting a selected subset of model objects. Microsoft explicitly states that perspectives are not a security feature, so a user with query access can still reference excluded or hidden columns.',
      'choice-c': 'A sensitivity label classifies the semantic model and can protect data in supported export paths. A label by itself does not remove in-service query permission from selected columns. Protection policies can restrict access to an entire labeled item, but they do not replace column-level OLS for this requirement.'
    },
    skillArea: 'Maintain Analytics / Security Boundaries (RLS vs OLS)',
    examTakeaway: 'RLS filters rows; OLS secures model objects (tables and columns). To prevent users from discovering or querying sensitive columns, implement Object-Level Security (OLS).'
