    correctChoiceId: 'choice-b',
    explanation: 'Fabric Git integration synchronizes supported workspace item definitions with source control. Deployment pipelines promote paired items through isolated stages, and deployment rules can replace supported data sources or parameters for a target stage. Production permissions remain a separate control and must prevent developers from bypassing the pipeline. Pull-request checks and unattended promotion require the appropriate external CI/CD process or Fabric APIs.',
    alternativeExplanations: {
      'choice-a': 'Git integration provides version history and synchronizes a workspace with a repository branch. Connecting Production directly to the development branch does not create a tested promotion path or apply target-stage deployment rules, so a committed but defective definition can still overwrite Production.',
      'choice-c': 'A deployment pipeline can promote paired items from Development to Test and Production. Workspace Members retain permission to modify Production content, however, so they can still bypass the pipeline by publishing or overwriting items manually.',
      'choice-d': 'Separate workspaces provide some environment isolation, but manual PBIX movement does not provide Git-backed item history, deployment-pipeline pairing, or governed deployment rules. Manual uploads can therefore reintroduce source-control gaps and environment configuration drift.'
    },
